From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Johannes Stein <johannesstein@freeze-dev.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 05:31:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 131/135] aarch64: a tbz or tbnz whose label is out of its
 reach becomes the opposite test over a b

tbz and tbnz reach +-32 KiB. The peephole optimizer forms them from a test of one bit and a b.eq or
b.ne without knowing how far the label is, so in a procedure larger than that the assembler refused
one that jumped across most of it ("conditional branch out of range"). After the optimizer, every
tbz or tbnz whose label is further than 7000 instructions away becomes the opposite test over an
unconditional branch, which reaches +-128 MiB:

  tbz reg,#bit,far   ->   tbnz reg,#bit,near
                          b    far
                        near:

The pass repeats until none is out of reach, since each rewrite moves the labels after it.
tests/test/opt/ttbz1.pp tests bit 3 across about 300 KiB of code.
